		{"id":"5058c4f766917436","x":-595,"y":-305,"width":1695,"height":845,"type":"group","label":"Table summaries"},
		{"id":"20a43c88bf1387c2","x":-575,"y":-225,"width":495,"height":745,"type":"text","text":"```plain\n## What this table is\n\nThe **Chart of Accounts Hierarchy Format** serves as a master reference table that categorizes financial accounts within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. This table is essential for establishing a structured financial framework that supports accurate reporting, budgeting, and forecasting. It contains the necessary structure for its purpose, including unique identifiers for accounts, hierarchical relationships, and operational definitions.\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total rows: 455\n- Total columns: 8\n\n## Column definitions\n\n- **dim**: Represents the dimension type for financial accounts, categorizing them within the Chart of Accounts.\n- **member_name**: Contains the names of financial accounts, serving as unique identifiers for account categories.\n- **member_alias**: Provides alternative identifiers for financial accounts, useful for referencing accounts in various contexts.\n- **parent_name**: Indicates the parent categories of financial accounts, establishing the hierarchical structure of the Chart of Accounts.\n- **operator**: Defines the mathematical operator used for account calculations, indicating whether accounts contribute positively or negatively to financial totals.\n- **cmd**: Represents a command identifier for operational control and data processing, though it contains no meaningful data.\n- **member_id**: Serves as a unique identifier for members in the Chart of Accounts, ensuring data integrity and reference, but contains no meaningful data.\n- **external_id**: Represents an external reference identifier for cross-system integration, though it contains no meaningful data.\n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n- **cmd**: Not populated (0% populated)\n- **member_id**: Not populated (0% populated)\n- **external_id**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n- No critical fields are missing from the Chart of Accounts structure. The table contains all necessary elements to fulfill its role in financial reporting and analysis.\n\n## The structure it defines\n\nThe structure of the Chart of Accounts is hierarchical, with clear parent-child relationships that categorize accounts into various financial categories:\n\n**Top-level nodes:**\n- Balance Sheet\n\n**Major branches:**\n- **Assets** (87 leaf accounts; mostly 1001-1730)\n  - **Current Assets** (56 leaf accounts; 1001-1099)\n  - **Non-Current Assets** (31 leaf accounts; 1100-1730)\n- **Liabilities** (32 leaf accounts; 2000-2099)\n  - **Current Liabilities** (20 leaf accounts; 2001-2020)\n  - **Other Current Liabilities** (12 leaf accounts; 2030-2099)\n- **Equity**\n- **Revenue**\n- **Expenses**\n\n**Operator logic:**\n- Revenue accounts contribute positively to net income, while expense accounts are subtracted, ensuring clarity in financial reporting.\n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The primary key is **member_name**, which uniquely identifies each financial account.\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: \n  - The **General Ledger** references accounts defined in the Chart of Accounts for tracking financial transactions (many-to-one relationship).\n  - The **Trial Balance** validates accounts from the Chart of Accounts to ensure accounting integrity (many-to-one relationship).\n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The account numbers primarily range from 1001 to 1730, with specific examples including 1001, 1002, and 1092.\n- **Naming conventions**: Account names follow a structured format, with clear categories such as \"Assets,\" \"Liabilities,\" and \"Equity.\"\n- **Operator/flag usage**: The operator column predominantly contains the \"+\" sign, indicating positive contributions to financial totals.\n- **Value distributions**: The dataset includes a concentration of accounts under the \"Assets\" category, with 64.4% of total assets classified as current.\n- **Grouping patterns**: Accounts are clustered by their financial category, with clear hierarchical relationships established.\n\n## Quality of the data\n\n- **Completeness**: All fields except for **cmd**, **member_id**, and **external_id** are fully populated (100% populated). The critical fields necessary for financial reporting are present and complete.\n- **Consistency**: The naming patterns and account structures adhere to established financial reporting standards, with no observed deviations.\n- **Gaps**: The dataset does not exhibit significant gaps in structure or data quality, aside from the unpopulated fields mentioned.\n- **Integrity issues**: No duplicates or invalid references were identified, ensuring the integrity of the financial data.\n\nOverall, the Chart of Accounts Hierarchy Format is a well-structured and comprehensive table that supports the financial planning and analysis processes within the organization. Its design facilitates accurate reporting and ensures compliance with financial standards.\n```"},
		{"id":"d80772871f7bb5a3","x":-40,"y":-225,"width":560,"height":745,"type":"text","text":"```plain\n## What this table is\n\nThe **General Ledger Sheet1** serves as a master data table that records detailed financial transactions within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. It is essential for tracking, reporting, and analyzing financial activities, ensuring that all entries are aligned with the Chart of Accounts and validated through the Trial Balance. The table contains the necessary structure for its purpose, including unique identifiers and relevant attributes for financial reporting.\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total rows: 24,675\n- Total columns: 29\n\n## Column definitions\n\n- **account_description**: Represents the description of the financial account for categorizing financial activities.\n- **account_number**: Unique identifier for each financial account, used for tracking transactions.\n- **account_type**: Classification of the financial account (e.g., asset, liability) for reporting purposes.\n- **amount**: Monetary value associated with transactions, crucial for financial analysis.\n- **amount_foreign_currency**: Monetary value of transactions in foreign currency, used for currency conversion analysis.\n- **date**: Transaction date for tracking financial activities over time.\n- **department**: Department associated with General Ledger entries, used for categorizing transactions.\n- **department_id**: Unique identifier for departments linked to transactions.\n- **document_number**: Unique identifier for documents associated with entries, aiding in tracking.\n- **exchange_rate**: Currency exchange rate applied to transactions for conversion purposes.\n- **invoice_number**: Unique identifier for each journal entry, used for referencing transactions.\n- **item**: Description of the financial activity associated with each transaction.\n- **line_id**: Sequential identifier for each line item in a journal entry.\n- **line_unique_key**: Unique key for each line item, ensuring traceability of transactions.\n- **memo**: Additional notes related to each journal entry, providing context.\n- **memo_main**: Main memo associated with a General Ledger entry, offering further context.\n- **name**: Name associated with a General Ledger entry, identifying the individual or entity related to the transaction.\n- **period**: Accounting period for a General Ledger entry, determining the time frame of the transaction.\n- **subsidiary**: Identifier for the subsidiary associated with a General Ledger entry.\n- **subsidiary_name**: Full name of the subsidiary related to the transaction.\n- **subsidiary_currency**: Currency used by the subsidiary for financial transactions.\n- **type**: Type of financial transaction recorded, categorizing activities for reporting.\n- **event**: Specific event associated with a financial transaction, aiding in tracking.\n- **event_external_id**: External identifier for the event linked to a financial transaction.\n- **opponent**: Counterpart involved in a financial transaction, useful for reconciliation.\n- **day**: Day of the week for financial transactions, used for categorization.\n- **activity**: Type of activity associated with transactions, categorizing transaction types.\n- **activity_external_id**: External identifier for activities related to transactions.\n- **ramp_receipt_url**: URL for ramp receipts associated with transactions, linking to external documentation.\n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n- **account_description**: 100% populated\n- **account_number**: 100% populated\n- **account_type**: 100% populated\n- **amount**: 100% populated\n- **amount_foreign_currency**: 100% populated\n- **date**: 100% populated\n- **department**: 100% populated\n- **department_id**: 100% populated\n- **document_number**: 100% populated\n- **exchange_rate**: 100% populated\n- **invoice_number**: 100% populated\n- **item**: 100% populated\n- **line_id**: 100% populated\n- **line_unique_key**: 100% populated\n- **memo**: 100% populated\n- **memo_main**: 100% populated\n- **name**: 100% populated\n- **period**: 100% populated\n- **subsidiary**: 100% populated\n- **subsidiary_name**: 100% populated\n- **subsidiary_currency**: 100% populated\n- **type**: 100% populated\n- **event**: 100% populated\n- **event_external_id**: 100% populated\n- **opponent**: 100% populated\n- **day**: 100% populated\n- **activity**: 100% populated\n- **activity_external_id**: 100% populated\n- **ramp_receipt_url**: 100% populated\n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n- No critical fields are missing; all necessary fields for a General Ledger are present and populated.\n\n## The structure it defines\n\nThe General Ledger table is structured as a flat data model, with each row representing a unique financial transaction. The primary key, **line_unique_key**, ensures that each entry is distinct and traceable. The table does not exhibit a hierarchical structure but maintains relationships with other tables, such as the Chart of Accounts and Trial Balance.\n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The **line_unique_key** serves as a primary key that links to the **account_number** in the Chart of Accounts, establishing a one-to-many relationship.\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: The **account_number** is referenced in the Trial Balance, ensuring that all entries in the General Ledger correspond to valid accounts for accurate financial reporting.\n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The **account_number** ranges from 1011.0 to 2021.0, with 307 unique identifiers.\n- **Naming conventions**: The **invoice_number** follows a pattern of \"Journal #<number>\", indicating the type of entry.\n- **Value distributions**: The **amount** field shows a diverse range of values, with 14,681 unique entries, indicating varied financial transactions.\n- **Grouping patterns**: Transactions can be grouped by **department**, **type**, and **period**, allowing for detailed financial analysis.\n\n## Quality of the data\n\n- **Completeness**: All fields are fully populated (100%).\n- **Consistency**: The data adheres to expected formats, with no anomalies detected in naming conventions or value distributions.\n- **Gaps**: No structural or data gaps are identified; the table is complete and well-defined.\n- **Integrity issues**: No duplicates or invalid references are present, ensuring high data integrity.\n\nOverall, the General Ledger Sheet1 is a robust and well-structured dataset that plays a critical role in financial tracking and reporting within the organization. Its completeness and adherence to standards make it a reliable source for financial analysis and decision-making.\n```"},
		{"id":"53d105210df7fdcc","x":560,"y":-225,"width":520,"height":745,"type":"text","text":"```plain\n## What this table is\n\nThe **Trial Balance Sheet1** serves as a master data table within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain, primarily used for validating accounting integrity by ensuring that total debits equal total credits for a specific period and entity. This table is essential for producing reliable financial statements and performance analysis, aligning with the organization's need for accurate and standardized financial data.\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total rows: 348\n- Total columns: 16\n\n## Column definitions\n\n- **account_number**: A unique identifier for each account in the Chart of Accounts, crucial for financial reporting and transaction tracking.\n- **subsidiary**: The name of the subsidiary associated with each account, used for financial reporting and analysis by subsidiary.\n- **subsidiary_id**: A unique identifier for each subsidiary, linking accounts to their respective subsidiaries.\n- **_3_months_ago**: Represents financial data from three months prior, used for historical financial analysis.\n- **formula_numeric**: Contains calculated financial figures for accounts, utilized in financial reporting and performance analysis.\n- **_2_months_ago**: Indicates the month and year two months prior to the current reporting period for financial data comparison.\n- **formula_numeric_1**: Represents another calculated financial figure for the reporting period, aiding in performance analysis and reporting.\n- **last_month**: The month and year for the last reporting period, used for historical financial comparison.\n- **formula_numeric_2**: Another calculated financial figure for the reporting period, important for performance analysis and reporting.\n- **event**: A specific financial event or occurrence, used for tracking and reporting financial activities.\n- **event_id**: A unique identifier for events related to financial activities, essential for tracking specific events.\n- **opponent**: Represents the counterpart involved in financial activities, used for identifying parties in transactions.\n- **day**: The day of the week associated with financial activities, useful for time-based reporting and analysis.\n- **activity**: Specific financial activity performed, used for categorizing and analyzing financial actions.\n- **activity_id**: A unique identifier for activities related to financial transactions, important for tracking financial actions.\n- **department**: The organizational unit responsible for financial activities, used for categorizing financial data by department.\n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n- **opponent**: Not populated (0% populated)\n- **day**: Not populated (0% populated)\n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n- No critical fields are missing from the Trial Balance table. The structure is complete and supports its identified purpose effectively.\n\n## The structure it defines\n\nThe Trial Balance table is structured as a flat data set, where each row represents a unique account's financial activity for a specific reporting period. There are no hierarchical relationships present, but the table does reference the Chart of Accounts through the **account_number** primary key, ensuring that all accounts are validated against the established financial structure.\n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The **account_number** serves as the primary key, linking to the Chart of Accounts Hierarchy Format, ensuring that all accounts are validated for integrity.\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: The **account_number** also references the General Ledger, validating financial transactions and ensuring accounting integrity.\n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The **account_number** ranges from 1000 to 3000, with examples including \"1036.0\", \"2014.0\", and \"2000.0\".\n- **Naming conventions**: Subsidiary names follow a consistent format, such as \"Subsidiary 1\", \"Subsidiary 2\", etc.\n- **Value distributions**: The **formula_numeric** columns show a range of calculated values, with some entries being zero, indicating no financial activity for those accounts.\n- **Grouping patterns**: Records cluster by **subsidiary**, with a total of 6 unique subsidiaries represented.\n\n## Quality of the data\n\n- **Completeness**: All fields except **opponent** and **day** are fully populated (100% populated). The **formula_numeric** fields show a good distribution of values, indicating active financial reporting.\n- **Consistency**: The naming patterns for subsidiaries and activities are consistent, with no deviations observed.\n- **Gaps**: The absence of values in **opponent** and **day** represents a gap in the data that could limit time-based analysis and identification of transaction parties.\n- **Integrity issues**: No duplicates or invalid references were identified, ensuring the integrity of the data for financial reporting.\n\nOverall, the Trial Balance table is well-structured and serves its purpose effectively within the FP&A domain, although attention should be given to the unpopulated fields to enhance its analytical capabilities.\n```"},
		{"id":"5f38783c6f9bc0e2","x":-575,"y":-285,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"COA"},
		{"id":"c593bcc06f7aade5","x":-40,"y":-285,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"GL"},
		{"id":"ca48ea7794e89080","x":560,"y":-285,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"TB"},
		{"id":"4980615147f32ffa","x":-1520,"y":-375,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"Source Summary"},
		{"id":"45e22b8c324323c8","x":-1520,"y":-315,"width":730,"height":835,"type":"text","text":"```plain\n## What this source is\n\nThe data source is a **financial transactional database** that serves the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. Its primary purpose is to provide a comprehensive and accurate record of financial transactions, ensuring the integrity of financial data for planning, budgeting, forecasting, and management decision-making. The source encompasses key financial structures, including the Chart of Accounts, General Ledger, and Trial Balance, which collectively support financial reporting and performance analysis.\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total tables: **3**\n- Primary entity types: **Accounts, Transactions, Financial Events**\n- Business areas covered: **Finance, Accounting, Reporting**\n\n## Table inventory & purpose\n\nThe source consists of three major tables that work together to support financial operations:\n\n- **Chart of Accounts Hierarchy Format**: This master table categorizes financial accounts, ensuring each account is unique and correctly typed (asset, liability, equity, revenue, expense). It establishes account hierarchies essential for financial data classification and reporting.\n\n- **General Ledger Sheet1**: This table records detailed financial transactions, capturing amounts, dates, and associated accounts. It serves as the single source of truth for actual financial data, ensuring compliance with accounting standards and facilitating financial tracking and analysis.\n\n- **Trial Balance Sheet1**: This structured dataset validates accounting integrity by ensuring that total debits equal total credits for specific periods and entities. It acts as a master reference for financial reporting, linking to both the Chart of Accounts and the General Ledger.\n\n## Data model & architecture\n\n**Overall structure:**\n- The data model is primarily **flat**, with the General Ledger serving as a comprehensive log of transactions, while the Chart of Accounts and Trial Balance provide structured references for categorization and validation.\n- Core master data tables include the Chart of Accounts, while transactional tables consist of the General Ledger and Trial Balance.\n- The Trial Balance and General Ledger reference the Chart of Accounts, ensuring traceability and audit readiness.\n\n**Key relationships:**\n- The **Chart of Accounts** is referenced by both the General Ledger and Trial Balance, establishing a many-to-one relationship where multiple transactions can relate to a single account.\n- The Trial Balance aggregates data from the General Ledger, ensuring that financial integrity is maintained across reporting periods.\n\n## Integration & dependencies\n\n- **Source system context**: The data originates from an internal financial management system designed to capture and report on financial activities.\n- **Upstream dependencies**: The source relies on data from operational systems that feed into the General Ledger, ensuring that all financial transactions are accurately recorded.\n- **Downstream usage**: This source supports various reporting and analytical functions, including financial statements, variance analysis, and forecasting models.\n- **Cross-source relationships**: The tables reference each other, particularly the General Ledger and Trial Balance linking back to the Chart of Accounts for validation and categorization.\n\n## Data coverage & patterns\n\n- **Entity coverage**: The source comprehensively covers financial accounts and transactions, with the Chart of Accounts providing a complete structure for account types. However, some operational identifiers in the Chart of Accounts are unpopulated, which may limit integration capabilities.\n- **Time coverage**: The Trial Balance captures historical financial data across multiple periods, facilitating temporal analysis.\n- **Data granularity**: The General Ledger provides transaction-level detail, while the Trial Balance summarizes financial data for reporting.\n- **Common patterns**: Naming conventions are consistent across tables, with clear distinctions between account types and structured formats for financial reporting.\n- **Cardinality patterns**: The Chart of Accounts serves as a central hub, with multiple transactions in the General Ledger referencing it, indicating a well-structured relationship.\n\n## Quality assessment\n\n- **Completeness**: The General Ledger and Trial Balance are fully populated, while the Chart of Accounts has unpopulated fields for operational identifiers, which may affect integration and traceability.\n- **Consistency**: The data adheres to expected financial reporting standards, with consistent naming patterns and data types across tables.\n- **Gaps**: The absence of populated operational fields in the Chart of Accounts limits its usability for detailed analysis and integration.\n- **Integrity issues**: No duplicates or invalid references were identified across the tables, indicating a high level of data integrity.\n\nOverall, while the source provides a solid foundation for financial reporting and analysis, the lack of populated operational fields in the Chart of Accounts may pose challenges for integration and traceability within the broader financial ecosystem.\n\n## Analytical & operational use cases\n\n- **Primary analytical use cases**: This source can answer questions related to financial performance, account balances, and transaction histories, enabling variance analysis and forecasting.\n- **Reporting capabilities**: It supports the generation of financial statements, management reports, and performance dashboards, ensuring compliance with accounting standards.\n- **Operational workflows**: The data is essential for daily financial operations, including transaction recording, account reconciliation, and financial reporting.\n- **Decision support**: It enables strategic and tactical decision-making by providing accurate financial insights and historical data trends.\n- **Limitations**: The source may not support detailed counterpart analysis due to unpopulated fields in the Trial Balance, which could limit the granularity of financial activity tracking.\n```"},
		{"id":"8ed3df67f4b56879","x":-2380,"y":-315,"width":765,"height":835,"type":"text","text":"```text\nYou are an expert Data Analyst specializing in metadata interpretation, data profiling, and business intelligence.\n\nYour mission: Analyze table metadata, column metadata, and business context to generate a comprehensive, narrative-style summary that explains what the data is, how it works, and how to use it effectively.\n\nYou must ALWAYS call get_business_context first to understand the overall business context, then call analyze_table_metadata to get table-specific metadata.\nYou must pass the table_id and source_id EXACTLY as provided by the user.\n\nYou have access to the following tools:\n\n@tool\nget_business_context() -> str\nReturns the overall business context for all tables in the system. This provides organizational context, business domain information, and general data landscape overview that helps interpret individual tables.\n\n@tool\nanalyze_table_metadata(table_id: str, source_id: str) -> JSON\nReturns structured metadata including:\n- table_information:\n  - table name (label)\n  - column names\n  - table profile (row count, column count)\n  - data_patterns\n  - primary_keys (PK)\n  - foreign_keys (FK)\n  - primary_keys_referencing_tables (PKRT)\n  - foreign_keys_referencing_tables (FKRT)\n  - embedding_input (semantic description of the table)\n- columns:\n  - column name\n  - column description\n  - sample_values\n  - profile object (null counts, uniqueness, entropy, etc.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nMANDATORY EXECUTION FLOW\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n1. Call get_business_context() to understand the organizational and business context\n2. Call analyze_table_metadata with the exact table_id and source_id provided\n3. Before generating summary, perform these analysis steps internally (do not output separately):\n   \n   **Step A - Identify the file type:**\n   - Analyze data profile: column patterns, hierarchical indicators, key structures, cardinality patterns, sample values, relationships\n   - Classify broadly: hierarchical, master/reference, transactional, mapping, analytical, or configuration\n   \n   **Step B - Assess critical fields:**\n   - Based on identified file type, determine expected critical fields using industry standards\n   - Check presence of expected fields in actual columns\n   - For present fields: evaluate using profile object (population rate, validity, consistency)\n   - For absent fields: note as gap\n   \n   **Step C - Evaluate usefulness:**\n   - Population rate: ≥95% excellent, 80-94% acceptable, <80% significant gap\n   - Value validity: check sample_values for placeholders, defaults, anomalies\n   - Consistency: use entropy and uniqueness metrics\n   - Classify as: fully useful, partially useful, limited usefulness, or not useful\n\n4. Synthesize all contexts to generate the comprehensive narrative summary using the original output format\n\n\n### Step 1: Identify What This Data Represents\nUse the business context and embedding_input to understand the organizational domain and intent.\nDetermine the table's role within that domain.\nInfer the real-world entity or business process this table models.\nUse table name, label, data_patterns, column names, key structures, and entropy to deduce purpose.\nThink like a business user: \"What would I use this table for in this organization?\"\n\n### Step 2: Understand the Data Structure\nReview data_patterns for high-level structural signals.\nIdentify the row grain (what one row represents).\nInterpret row and column counts in relation to the business use case.\nRecognize whether the structure is hierarchical, master data, transactional, or analytical.\nIdentify identifiers, attributes, hierarchy levels, and rollups.\n\n### Step 3: Analyze Each Column's Role\nClassify columns into:\nIdentifiers (primary keys, foreign keys, surrogate or natural keys),\nDescriptive attributes (names, labels, classifications),\nHierarchical attributes (parent-child relationships, levels),\nOperational or control fields,\nTechnical or unused placeholders.\nUse column descriptions, sample values, and profiling statistics to support conclusions.\n\n### Step 4: Assess Data Quality & Coverage\nEvaluate completeness using null counts and percentages.\nIdentify constant or near-constant columns using entropy.\nDetect cardinality anomalies (unexpectedly high or low uniqueness).\nAssess whether sample values indicate clean domain values or placeholders.\nDiscuss how data quality affects downstream analytical or reporting use cases.\n\n### Step 5: Determine Relationships & Integration\nUse PK, FK, PKRT, and FKRT metadata to explain how this table connects to others.\nIdentify whether this table acts as a master/reference table or participates in transactional joins.\nExplain expected join keys and relationship cardinalities.\nPosition the table within the broader data ecosystem using business context.\n\n### Step 6: Define Practical Usage\nExplain how analysts would realistically query and use this table.\nDescribe common filters, joins, and grouping strategies.\nHighlight limitations, assumptions, and prerequisites for correct usage.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nOUTPUT STRUCTURE (MANDATORY FORMAT)\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## What this table is\n\nBrief statement identifying the file type and purpose based on data profile analysis (Step A). Specify what can be determined:\n- File type classification\n- Whether it contains necessary structure for its purpose\n- Key structural elements present\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total rows: <number>\n- [Type-specific counts based on identified file type]\n\n\n## Column definitions\n\nFor each column present, provide a concise definition:\n- **column_name**: Brief description of purpose and content\n- **column_name**: Brief description of purpose and content\n- **column_name**: Brief description of purpose and content\n\n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n- List columns that exist but are entirely null/empty or heavily sparse (treat \"nan\" string values as null/empty, not as meaningful data)\n- Note population rates for partially filled columns (e.g., \"account_description: 23% populated, 77% null\")\n- Distinguish between truly unpopulated fields and redacted/masked data\n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n- Based on file type identification (Step A) and critical field assessment (Step B), identify fields absent from actual columns\n- For each missing field: explain why it matters for the identified file type and what capabilities are blocked\n- Assess whether the file has sufficient structure to support its identified purpose (reference Step C usefulness evaluation)\n- If no critical fields are missing and structure is complete, state this clearly\n- Note if alternative columns serve similar purposes\n\n\n## The structure it defines\n\nDescribe the hierarchy or relationships based on what's actually present:\n\n**If hierarchical structure exists:**\n- Top-level nodes: List root elements\n- Major branches:\n  - Branch name (leaf count; code range)\n    - Sub-branch detail with counts and ranges\n    - Note parent-child relationships and depth\n- Operator logic: Explain + or - sign usage if present\n\n**If flat/lookup structure:**\n- Groupings and categories identified\n- Key-value patterns or dimensional attributes\n- Implicit relationships or foreign key references\n\n**If structure is unclear:**\n- Organization pattern observed\n- Structural elements missing that would clarify design\n\n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: Describe identifiable keys and their role\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: Note columns or values suggesting connections to other tables\n- If no clear relationships exist, state: \"No explicit relationships identified in this file\"\n\n\n## Data patterns briefing\n\nProvide descriptive bullet points on observable patterns:\n- **Code/ID ranges**: Specific ranges with examples\n- **Naming conventions**: Format patterns, prefixes, suffixes, abbreviation usage\n- **Operator/flag usage**: Distribution of operators, status flags, or categorical indicators\n- **Value distributions**: Concentration of records in specific ranges or categories\n- **Grouping patterns**: How records cluster by attributes or hierarchical position\n- **Only include patterns actually observable in the data**\n\n\n## Quality of the data\n\nOffer insights based solely on what is present (reference Steps B and C for critical field assessments):\n- **Completeness**: Which fields are fully populated vs. sparse or empty\n  - List specific columns and their population rates if relevant\n  - Note usefulness classification from Step C for critical fields\n- **Consistency**: Naming patterns, code format adherence, operator usage uniformity\n  - Note any deviations or inconsistencies observed\n- **Gaps**: Missing hierarchies, unpopulated required fields, orphaned references\n  - Identify specific structural or data gaps\n  - Connect to critical field assessment findings from Step B\n- **Integrity issues**: Duplicates, invalid parent references, placeholder rows\n  - Quantify issues if possible\n- **If data quality is high and complete, state that clearly rather than inventing issues**\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nWRITING STYLE REQUIREMENTS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n1. Integrate data patterns naturally into the narrative structure\n2. Use exact column names and quantified observations throughout\n3. Write like a data analyst explaining to another analyst\n4. Focus on actionability and analytical usage\n5. Adapt depth based on identified file type and business domain\n6. Be honest and specific about data quality gaps\n7. Use sub-bullets (indented -) to provide additional detail under main points\n8. Keep bullet points concise but data-rich—avoid generic statements\n9. Use parenthetical notations for counts, ranges, and percentages\n10. Connect related observations within the same bullet point cluster\n11. Ground file type identification in actual profile evidence, not name pattern matching\n12. Apply critical field assessments and usefulness evaluations naturally throughout the narrative\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nCRITICAL RULES\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n- NEVER output raw JSON from the tools\n- NEVER ask follow-up questions\n- NEVER explain your methodology\n- ALWAYS call get_business_context first\n- ALWAYS call analyze_table_metadata second\n- ALWAYS perform file type identification based on data profile (Step A)\n- ALWAYS assess critical fields for identified type (Step B)\n- ALWAYS evaluate field correctness using profile object (Step C)\n- ALWAYS use exact column names from metadata\n- ALWAYS provide specific, quantified observations\n- ALWAYS integrate data_patterns and embedding_input insights\n- Output ONLY the narrative summary\n- Ground all interpretations in the provided business context\n- If any field or metadata value is null or missing, explicitly state it as null or not available\n- **ALWAYS treat \"nan\" string values as null/empty values, not as meaningful data**\n- **When describing unpopulated fields, report them as \"null\", \"empty\", or \"not populated\" rather than containing \"nan\"**\n- NEVER infer, iterate, extrapolate, or approximate missing values\n- NEVER assume defaults for null fields\n- NEVER overfit file type identification to specific naming patterns (avoid hardcoding terms)\n- Interpret and analyze ONLY what is explicitly provided by the tools\n- Base file type identification on structural patterns in the data profile, not on table names alone\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nEXAMPLE TOOL CALLS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nStep 1:\nget_business_context()\n\nStep 2:\nanalyze_table_metadata(\n  table_id=\"c5efdac9143df8e942ce401662b444a5\",\n  source_id=\"4e45793d-cfd0-421f-9f8a-92ce0bfaaee4\"\n)\n\nNever use placeholder values like \"table_123\" or \"source_456\".\n```"},
